WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.120
Randomized quicksort has

00:00:02.196 --> 00:00:03.713
two practical costs.

00:00:03.713 --> 00:00:06.596
Random numbers take time to generate

00:00:06.672 --> 00:00:09.782
and a program that behaves differently on

00:00:09.858 --> 00:00:12.740
every run is harder to test and debug.

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:14.366
Weiss's textbook

00:00:14.441 --> 00:00:16.618
Data Structures and Algorithm

00:00:16.693 --> 00:00:19.095
Analysis in Java, chapter seven

00:00:19.095 --> 00:00:22.023
gives a version that is deterministic

00:00:22.023 --> 00:00:24.050
and very good in practice.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.603
It differs from the randomized

00:00:26.676 --> 00:00:28.292
version in three ways

00:00:28.292 --> 00:00:30.128
how the pivot is chosen

00:00:30.128 --> 00:00:31.670
how partition scans

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:34.240
and what happens for small ranges.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:39.858
Here is the plan, on the slides' example

00:00:39.858 --> 00:00:45.020
8, 1, 4, 9, 6, 3, 5

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:47.232
2, 7, 0.

00:00:47.232 --> 00:00:49.567
Look at the first

00:00:49.567 --> 00:00:54.359
center and last elements of the range

00:00:54.359 --> 00:00:56.940
8, 6 and 0.

00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:59.596
The pivot is their median, 6.

00:00:59.671 --> 00:01:02.870
The first element alone would be a terrible

00:01:02.944 --> 00:01:05.251
pivot for already-sorted input

00:01:05.325 --> 00:01:08.376
the median of three is never the smallest

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:10.980
or the largest value in the range.

00:01:11.054 --> 00:01:12.988
Then hide the pivot at the

00:01:13.063 --> 00:01:15.890
second-last position, right - 1.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.543
Two pointers: i starts at the second element

00:01:19.618 --> 00:01:20.819
and moves right

00:01:20.819 --> 00:01:23.971
skipping elements smaller than the pivot.

00:01:24.046 --> 00:01:25.998
j starts at the third-last

00:01:26.073 --> 00:01:27.800
element and moves left

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.877
skipping elements larger than the pivot.

00:01:30.877 --> 00:01:32.679
When both have stopped

00:01:32.679 --> 00:01:34.705
and they have not crossed

00:01:34.705 --> 00:01:37.182
swap the two elements and go on.

00:01:37.182 --> 00:01:40.560
When they cross, swap the pivot with a[i].

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:44.492
The method median3 does this.

00:01:44.492 --> 00:01:48.140
Center is (left + right) / 2.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:50.771
Three compare-and-swaps sort

00:01:50.852 --> 00:01:53.052
the three samples in place

00:01:53.052 --> 00:01:56.473
if a[center] is smaller than a[left]

00:01:56.473 --> 00:01:57.369
swap them

00:01:57.369 --> 00:02:00.709
if a[right] is smaller than a[left]

00:02:00.709 --> 00:02:03.968
swap them; and if a[right] is smaller

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:06.330
than a[center], swap them.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:07.884
Now a[left]

00:02:07.884 --> 00:02:11.412
a[center] and a[right] are in order

00:02:11.412 --> 00:02:13.992
so the median sits at center.

00:02:13.992 --> 00:02:16.917
It is swapped to right - 1

00:02:16.917 --> 00:02:19.240
and returned as the pivot.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:21.385
On our example.

00:02:21.385 --> 00:02:26.081
a[center], 6, is smaller than a[left]

00:02:26.081 --> 00:02:27.959
8: swap them.

00:02:27.959 --> 00:02:32.655
a[right], 0, is smaller than a[left]

00:02:32.655 --> 00:02:34.220
now 6: swap.

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:36.770
And a[right], now 6

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:40.620
is smaller than a[center], 8: swap.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:44.103
The samples are now 0, 6, 8.

00:02:44.103 --> 00:02:46.395
Finally the median, 6

00:02:46.395 --> 00:02:49.970
moves to index 8, right - 1.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:51.388
A small bonus

00:02:51.465 --> 00:02:54.364
a[left] is now at most the pivot and

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.729
a[right] at least the pivot

00:02:56.729 --> 00:02:59.934
so they are already on the correct sides

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.452
and partition only looks at

00:03:02.528 --> 00:03:04.665
left + 1 to right - 2.

00:03:04.665 --> 00:03:06.572
They are also sentinels

00:03:06.572 --> 00:03:08.938
j cannot run off the left end

00:03:08.938 --> 00:03:12.371
because it stops at a[left] at the latest

00:03:12.371 --> 00:03:14.355
and i stops at the pivot.

00:03:14.355 --> 00:03:17.560
So the inner loops need no bounds checks.

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:20.051
Here is the full method.

00:03:20.051 --> 00:03:23.555
If the range has more than CUTOFF elements

00:03:23.555 --> 00:03:26.740
median3 picks and hides the pivot.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:29.212
Then i starts at left and

00:03:29.297 --> 00:03:31.080
j at right - 1.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:34.560
Each inner loop first moves its pointer

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:38.295
then tests: ++i, and --j.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:41.690
So the first elements examined are

00:03:41.775 --> 00:03:44.237
left + 1 and right - 2

00:03:44.237 --> 00:03:45.595
as in the plan

00:03:45.595 --> 00:03:49.330
and after every swap both pointers move on.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:51.426
If i is at least j

00:03:51.426 --> 00:03:54.550
the pointers have crossed: break.

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:57.674
Otherwise swap a[i] and a[j]

00:03:57.674 --> 00:03:58.960
and continue.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:02.611
After the loop, swap the pivot back from

00:04:02.694 --> 00:04:05.830
right - 1 to i, its final place.

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:10.438
Then the two sides are sorted recursively

00:04:10.438 --> 00:04:12.452
left to i - 1

00:04:12.452 --> 00:04:14.850
and i + 1 to right.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.150
And small ranges go to insertion sort.

00:04:18.227 --> 00:04:20.478
For tiny ranges the recursion

00:04:20.556 --> 00:04:22.496
costs more than the work

00:04:22.574 --> 00:04:25.291
and insertion sort is fast on small

00:04:25.368 --> 00:04:27.231
or nearly sorted arrays.

00:04:27.231 --> 00:04:28.473
The test means

00:04:28.473 --> 00:04:31.656
the range has more than CUTOFF elements.

00:04:31.656 --> 00:04:34.528
The textbook uses 10; we use 3

00:04:34.528 --> 00:04:37.633
so the small examples really partition.

00:04:37.633 --> 00:04:39.573
It must be at least 2

00:04:39.573 --> 00:04:41.436
on a 2-element range

00:04:41.436 --> 00:04:43.920
j would step outside the range.

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:45.562
One more detail.

00:04:45.643 --> 00:04:48.309
Both inner loops stop on elements

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:49.924
equal to the pivot

00:04:49.924 --> 00:04:52.994
they test less than and greater than

00:04:52.994 --> 00:04:54.528
not less-or-equal.

00:04:54.609 --> 00:04:56.790
That costs some unnecessary

00:04:56.871 --> 00:04:58.486
swaps on duplicates

00:04:58.567 --> 00:05:01.394
but i and j then meet in the middle

00:05:01.475 --> 00:05:03.333
of a run of equal keys

00:05:03.414 --> 00:05:06.483
so an array of equal elements is split

00:05:06.564 --> 00:05:09.310
evenly and still sorts in O(n log n).

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:13.766
Let us run the first partition.

00:05:13.766 --> 00:05:19.142
After median3: 0, 1, 4, 9

00:05:19.142 --> 00:05:23.768
7, 3, 5, 2, 6, 8

00:05:23.768 --> 00:05:29.019
with the pivot 6 hidden at index 8.

00:05:29.019 --> 00:05:32.770
i starts at 0, j at 8.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:36.541
i skips 1 and 4, and stops at 9

00:05:36.541 --> 00:05:37.653
index 3.

00:05:37.653 --> 00:05:40.818
j stops at once at 2, index 7

00:05:40.818 --> 00:05:43.128
which is smaller than 6.

00:05:43.128 --> 00:05:46.720
They have not crossed: swap 9 and 2.

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:51.648
i skips 2 and stops at 7, index 4

00:05:51.648 --> 00:05:56.326
j skips 9 and stops at 5, index 6.

00:05:56.326 --> 00:05:58.610
Swap 7 and 5.

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:02.698
i skips 5 and 3 and stops at 7

00:06:02.698 --> 00:06:06.811
index 6; j skips 7 and stops at 3

00:06:06.811 --> 00:06:07.908
index 5.

00:06:07.908 --> 00:06:10.192
Now i is greater than j

00:06:10.192 --> 00:06:12.660
the pointers have crossed.

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:14.430
Swap a[i], 7

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:16.910
with the pivot at index 8.

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:20.270
The pivot 6 is at index 6, with

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:23.630
0, 1, 4, 2, 5, 3, all smaller,

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:26.030
on its left, and 9, 7, 8

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:27.630
on its right.

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:32.139
An exercise from the slides: sort

00:06:32.139 --> 00:06:35.664
5, 7, 4, 1, 0, 2, 9.

00:06:35.664 --> 00:06:39.593
The first step is to select the pivot.

00:06:39.593 --> 00:06:42.716
Which value becomes the pivot

00:06:42.716 --> 00:06:45.133
and where is it hidden?

00:06:45.133 --> 00:06:47.450
Pause and work it out.

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:55.092
The samples are a[0], 5; a[3], 1;

00:06:55.092 --> 00:06:57.400
and a[6], 9.

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:01.555
median3 swaps 5 and 1, giving

00:07:01.555 --> 00:07:04.879
1, 7, 4, 5, 0, 2, 9.

00:07:04.879 --> 00:07:06.910
So 5 is the pivot.

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:10.973
Then it hides 5 at index 5,

00:07:10.973 --> 00:07:15.475
right - 1: 1, 7, 4, 2,

00:07:15.475 --> 00:07:17.500
0, 5, 9.

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:20.143
Here is the whole sort

00:07:20.143 --> 00:07:22.653
with a print in every call

00:07:22.653 --> 00:07:24.268
and CUTOFF = 3.

00:07:24.268 --> 00:07:26.420
In the first partition

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:29.827
i stops at 7 and j at 0: swap.

00:07:29.827 --> 00:07:33.594
Then i stops at 7 again and j at 2

00:07:33.594 --> 00:07:37.360
crossed, so the pivot goes to index 4.

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:40.420
The left part, 1, 0, 4, 2,

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:43.506
has 4 elements, more than the cutoff

00:07:43.506 --> 00:07:45.613
so it is partitioned again.

00:07:45.613 --> 00:07:48.624
The median of 1, 0 and 2 is 1.

00:07:48.624 --> 00:07:50.430
1 lands at index 1.

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:53.130
Every other range is small enough

00:07:53.202 --> 00:07:55.837
for insertion sort: index 0 alone

00:07:55.837 --> 00:07:57.403
indices 2 to 3

00:07:57.403 --> 00:07:59.540
and 5 to 6 on the right.

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:02.283
The last line checks that sort also

00:08:02.351 --> 00:08:05.086
handles the first example: 0 to 9

00:08:05.086 --> 00:08:05.770
in order.

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:09.657
A pitfall: the slides write the partition

00:08:09.738 --> 00:08:11.109
loop differently.

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:13.610
They start with i at left + 1

00:08:13.691 --> 00:08:16.030
and j at right - 2

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:18.809
and the inner loops test first

00:08:18.809 --> 00:08:21.630
and move only while the test holds.

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:22.962
So after a swap

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.547
neither pointer moves unless the

00:08:25.626 --> 00:08:27.820
swapped values make it move.

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:30.719
With distinct elements that is fine

00:08:30.719 --> 00:08:34.010
after a swap, both tests succeed and both

00:08:34.088 --> 00:08:35.420
pointers move on.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:40.724
But take all-equal data: 5, 5, 5,

00:08:40.724 --> 00:08:42.074
5, 5.

00:08:42.074 --> 00:08:45.111
i starts at 1, j at 2.

00:08:45.111 --> 00:08:48.373
Both values equal the pivot

00:08:48.373 --> 00:08:50.960
so neither loop moves.

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:53.817
They have not crossed, so swap

00:08:53.817 --> 00:08:55.031
5 and 5.

00:08:55.031 --> 00:08:56.406
Nothing changes.

00:08:56.406 --> 00:08:58.913
Both loops stop at once again

00:08:58.913 --> 00:09:02.310
and the same swap happens again, forever.

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:04.681
The program in the notes

00:09:04.765 --> 00:09:06.533
adds a round counter

00:09:06.533 --> 00:09:08.553
after 1 million rounds

00:09:08.553 --> 00:09:11.500
i is still 1 and j is still 2.

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:14.905
The fix is the one in our method

00:09:14.905 --> 00:09:18.052
move the pointers before testing

00:09:18.145 --> 00:09:20.644
starting from i at left and

00:09:20.736 --> 00:09:22.680
j at right - 1

00:09:22.773 --> 00:09:25.550
or add i++ and j--

00:09:25.642 --> 00:09:27.586
after the swap.

00:09:27.679 --> 00:09:30.271
For distinct keys both forms

00:09:30.363 --> 00:09:32.770
do exactly the same steps.

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:34.264
To sum up.

00:09:34.264 --> 00:09:37.098
One: median-of-three takes the

00:09:37.189 --> 00:09:39.017
median of the first,

00:09:39.017 --> 00:09:42.582
center, and last elements as the pivot

00:09:42.582 --> 00:09:45.599
and hides it at right - 1.

00:09:45.599 --> 00:09:48.981
The sorted samples act as sentinels.

00:09:48.981 --> 00:09:52.363
Two: i and j move towards each other

00:09:52.454 --> 00:09:54.739
and swap misplaced pairs

00:09:54.739 --> 00:09:57.390
and they stop on equal keys.

00:09:57.740 --> 00:10:01.405
Three: ranges of at most CUTOFF elements

00:10:01.496 --> 00:10:03.420
go to insertion sort.

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:07.130
Try this: why must CUTOFF be at least 2?

00:10:07.210 --> 00:10:10.330
Trace median3 and the two pointers

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:12.570
on a range of 2 elements

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:14.330
and see where j goes.

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:17.368
Next: the running time of quicksort.

00:10:17.368 --> 00:10:19.683
The worst case, the best case,

00:10:19.683 --> 00:10:22.520
and why the expected time is n log n.

